---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Markdown Syntax Reference: Headings, Tables, Code & More | MarkdownMaster"
  description="Markdown syntax reference with copyable examples for headings, formatting, lists, code blocks, links, tables, and task lists. Practice each pattern in a free editor."
  canonical="https://markdownmaster.site/docs/"
>
  <style>
    .page-wrap { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1.5rem; }
    .page-wrap h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .page-wrap .subtitle { color: var(--text-dim); margin-bottom: 2rem; }
    .page-wrap section { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
    .page-wrap h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; color: var(--accent); }
    .page-wrap h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
    .page-wrap p { color: #cbd5e1; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; line-height: 1.8; }
    .page-wrap pre { background: #0f172a; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; overflow-x: auto; margin: 0.75rem 0; }
    .page-wrap p code { background: #1e293b; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px; color: #f8b4b4; font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.85rem; }
    .page-wrap table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0.75rem 0; }
    .page-wrap th, .page-wrap td { border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 8px 12px; text-align: left; }
    .page-wrap th { background: var(--bg-card); color: var(--text); }
    .page-wrap td { color: #cbd5e1; }
    .practice-panel { margin-top: 2.75rem; padding: 1.4rem; border: 1px solid rgba(14,165,233,.45); border-radius: 12px; background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(14,165,233,.12), rgba(15,23,42,.25)); }
    .practice-panel h2 { color: var(--text); margin: 0 0 .45rem; }
    .practice-panel p { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
    .practice-links { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr)); gap: .65rem; }
    .practice-link { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 120px; padding: .9rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 9px; background: var(--bg-card); color: var(--text); text-decoration: none; transition: transform .15s ease, border-color .15s ease; }
    .practice-link:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); border-color: var(--accent); }
    .practice-link:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
    .practice-link strong { margin-bottom: .35rem; font-size: .9rem; }
    .practice-link span { color: var(--text-dim); font-size: .82rem; line-height: 1.55; }
    @media (max-width: 680px) { .practice-links { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .practice-link { min-height: 0; } }
  </style>

  <div class="page-wrap">
    <h1>Markdown Syntax Reference</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Copyable examples for the Markdown patterns you use most, from headings and formatting to tables and task lists.</p>

    <section>
      <h2>Headings</h2>
      <p>Use <code>#</code> symbols to create headings. One <code>#</code> for the largest heading, up to six for the smallest.</p>
      <pre><code>{`# Heading 1
## Heading 2
### Heading 3
#### Heading 4
##### Heading 5
###### Heading 6`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section id="document-titles-and-headings" aria-labelledby="document-titles-and-headings-title">
      <h2 id="document-titles-and-headings-title">Markdown document titles and headings</h2>
      <p>
        Markdown does not define one universal document-title field. In a plain <code>.md</code> file or a GitHub README,
        start the visible document with one clear H1 heading. The H1 is the title readers see in the rendered document.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`# Project name`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Some publishing tools also read a <code>title</code> value in YAML front matter. That metadata can set a browser-tab
        title or generated page title, but it is tool-specific. It does not replace a visible H1 in every Markdown renderer.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`---
title: Project name
---

# Project name`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Use the H1 to name the document readers see, then use H2 and H3 headings to organize the sections that follow.
        Choose heading levels for their place in the outline, not only for visual size.
      </p>

      <h3>README example: fix the heading structure</h3>
      <p>
        This README has a missing space after the H1 marker and skips from the title directly to an H3 section:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`#Project name

### Install
npm install example-package`}</code></pre>
      <p>Use a space after the <code>#</code> marker and continue with the next level in the document outline:</p>
      <pre><code>{`# Project name

## Install
npm install example-package`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Before publishing, use the
        <a href="/tools/markdown-linter/?from=docs-title-check">Markdown Formatter &amp; Linter</a>
        to review heading spacing and possible skipped heading levels. It can apply deterministic spacing fixes after you
        review the diff; choices about a document's structure remain yours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Text Formatting</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Style</th><th scope="col">Syntax</th><th scope="col">Output</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Bold</td><td><code>**text**</code></td><td><strong>text</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Italic</td><td><code>*text*</code></td><td><em>text</em></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strikethrough</td><td><code>~~text~~</code></td><td><s>text</s></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inline Code</td><td><code>`code`</code></td><td><code>code</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Lists</h2>
      <p>Unordered lists use <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, or <code>+</code>.</p>
      <pre><code>{`- Item 1
- Item 2
  - Nested item`}</code></pre>
      <p>Ordered lists use numbers:</p>
      <pre><code>{`1. First
2. Second
3. Third`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Code Blocks</h2>
      <p>Wrap code in triple backticks with an optional language identifier for syntax highlighting:</p>
      <pre><code>{`\`\`\`javascript
function hello() {
  console.log('Hello!');
}
\`\`\``}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Links &amp; Images</h2>
      <pre><code>{`[Link text](https://example.com)
![Alt text](image-url.jpg)`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Blockquotes</h2>
      <pre><code>{`> This is a blockquote
> Multiple lines`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Tables</h2>
      <p>Use pipes to separate cells and hyphens to separate the header from the body. Need more rows or alignment controls? <a href="/tools/markdown-table-generator/?from=docs-table">Create a table with the generator</a>.</p>
      <pre><code>{`| Header 1 | Header 2 |
| -------- | -------- |
| Cell 1   | Cell 2   |`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Task Lists</h2>
      <pre><code>{`- [x] Completed task
- [ ] Uncompleted task`}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="practice-panel" aria-labelledby="practice-title">
      <h2 id="practice-title">Try a syntax example now</h2>
      <p>Choose the workflow that matches what you are making. No account is required, and your Markdown stays in your browser.</p>
      <div class="practice-links">
        <a class="practice-link" href="/editor/?from=docs-reference">
          <strong>Practice in the editor →</strong>
          <span>Paste an example, change it, and see the formatted result as you work.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="practice-link" href="/tools/markdown-table-generator/?from=docs-table">
          <strong>Build a Markdown table →</strong>
          <span>Edit cells and alignment without lining up pipes by hand.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="practice-link" href="/tools/readme-generator/?from=docs-readme">
          <strong>Start a GitHub README →</strong>
          <span>Turn your project details into a reusable Markdown structure.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="practice-link" href="/tools/markdown-linter/?from=docs-linter">
          <strong>Check Markdown formatting →</strong>
          <span>Review heading, list, whitespace, and code-fence checks before you publish.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
